\Section{Conditions for revisiting the decision}
Revisit the growth plan if later cohorts' six-month cash recovery falls toward the earlier window, if channel iCAC rises above the relevant contribution-value benchmark, or if young-cohort outcomes fall materially below forecast. Historical blended CAC does not establish the return from another advertising dollar.


\SectionDivider{01}{Studio North and the customer journey}{The membership business, customer needs and possible sources of value and friction.}
\PageTitle{01 / BUSINESS OVERVIEW}{The membership experience\newline behind the economics}{Studio North serves independent professionals seeking faster creative work or stronger skills.}
Templates support immediate work; guided lessons build capabilities over repeated use. A monthly membership is valuable when those benefits recur often enough to justify the next payment. Optional workshops and add-ons expand spending but do not by themselves establish durable membership value.
\ReportFigure{business-journey}{Conceptual. A broad conceptual journey through the Studio North membership. Benefits and friction points are possible experiences, not measured sentiment or population conversion rates.}{business-journey.png}{4.3in}
\Section{Potential sources of renewal friction}
An unclear plan choice can delay subscription. A difficult first task can prevent a member from experiencing value. Later, a lack of time or an unmet learning goal may weaken the reason to renew. These are business hypotheses for investigation; the source records do not establish their causes.
\Section{Evidence available to the management team}
The CRM records stated needs and baseline characteristics. Product events show activity, transactions show spending, renewal records show continuation, and feedback captures selected perceptions. Together, these sources support a more informative diagnosis than a renewal rate alone. Tables~\ref{tab:ucv-people}--\ref{tab:ucv-channel-holdouts} classify all eight sources using the Universal Customer View. The appendix documents parameters, validation and reproducibility.

\SectionDivider{02}{Channel holdouts and acquisition cost}{Establish the additional customers generated by each acquisition campaign before evaluating their long-run value.}
\PageTitle{02 / CHANNEL HOLDOUTS}{How many acquisitions\newline did the campaign create?}{Four channel--need tests | July 1--August 1, 2025 | End date exclusive.}
\Section{Design and measurement}
The acquisition pilots cover Search and Partner audiences within the two stated needs, save time and build skill. Each test randomly assigns 500 eligible, never-paid prospects to the focal campaign and 500 to its own holdout. People do not overlap across tests. The holdout withholds that campaign while ordinary background conditions continue. Acquisition is the first paid purchase through any route during the 31-day window; nonbuyers remain in the assignment totals.

The pilot summaries are a separate acquisition research sample and budget, outside the operational cohort panel. They contain assignment counts, acquisitions and acquisition costs. They provide no revenue or retention outcomes and do not enter the customer-value model.
\TableCaption{channel-buyers}{Descriptive acquisition results by randomized arm. T denotes treatment and C denotes control. Equal arm sizes make control acquisitions the estimated background at treatment scale.}
\VAR{channel_counts_table}
\Section{Background inflow and additional acquisitions}
The holdout estimates acquisitions that would occur without the focal campaign. This background can include prior brand exposure and other marketing; it is not a company-wide estimate of purely organic demand. With unequal arm sizes, control acquisitions must first be scaled by $k=N_T/N_C$.
\[
\text{Additional acquisitions }\Delta B=B_T-kB_C.
\]
\Evidence{Source: \Code{channel_holdouts.csv}; saved SQL result: \Code{channel_cac.csv}. Each channel has its own eligible audience and control, so these are within-channel effects rather than a randomized head-to-head channel comparison.}
\Takeaway{Campaign credit counts acquisitions associated with an intervention. The holdout identifies how many additional acquisitions it generated. Customer value is considered after the cohort analysis.}

\NewPage
\PageTitle{02 / INCREMENTAL CAC}{From credited acquisitions\newline to the cost of an additional customer}{Acquisition spending includes focal media and incremental campaign-specific creative/vendor costs.}
\Section{Two acquisition denominators}
For a transparent attribution-style benchmark, credit every treated buyer to the focal campaign. Credited CAC is treatment acquisition spending divided by treated buyers. Incremental CAC uses additional spending and additional acquisitions:
\[
\text{Credited CAC}=\frac{S_T}{B_T},\qquad
\mathrm{iCAC}=\frac{S_T-kS_C}{B_T-kB_C},\qquad k=\frac{N_T}{N_C}.
\]
\TableCaption{channel-cost}{Each treatment arm spends \$10,000 on media and \$1,000 on other incremental acquisition costs; its holdout spends zero on that focal campaign. Credited CAC and iCAC use the same cost scope.}
\VAR{channel_cost_table}
\VAR{channel_example}
\Section{Why the correction matters}
The distinction follows \SourceCite{larsen2019}{Larsen (2019)} and the acquisition cost-curve discussion in Managing Customer Data Topic 3, slides 6--10 (\SourceCite{course2026}{Tian, 2026}). Crediting background buyers to a campaign makes acquisition look cheaper than it is. The relevant cost for an expansion decision is the cost of generating additional customers.
\Section{What is established at this point}
The tests estimate iCAC at the recorded spending level and audience. Sampling uncertainty remains; Appendix A16 reports the acquisition-lift and iCAC intervals. These results do not yet rank investment opportunities: a customer acquired cheaply may also generate little contribution.
\Boundary{The closing acquisition-decision section pairs these iCAC estimates with contribution LTV before CAC. That planning step states its assumption about the value of additional customers explicitly. No pilot contribution, retention or lifetime treatment effect is estimated.}

