\SectionDivider{05}{Customer segmentation}{Find useful cells with similar outcomes within each cell and different outcomes across cells, then connect them to decisions.}

\PageTitle{05 / SEGMENTATION OBJECTIVE}{Similar within cells,\newline different across cells}{Compare candidate partitions on the same customers, outcome, horizon and number of cells.}
\Section{The objective is a useful partition of heterogeneity}
Choose a segmentation strategy that increases \textbf{within-cell homogeneity and across-cell heterogeneity} in a decision-relevant outcome. The course grouping exercise holds willingness-to-pay values and cell counts fixed while changing who is grouped together (\SourceCite{course2026}{Tian, 2026}, Topic 1, slides 37--39). Here the outcome is six-receipt contribution per originally acquired customer.
\[
\underbrace{\operatorname{Var}(Y)}_{\text{fixed total variation}}
=\underbrace{\sum_g w_g\operatorname{Var}(Y\mid g)}_{\text{within-cell variation}}
+\underbrace{\sum_g w_g(\bar Y_g-\bar Y)^2}_{\text{between-cell variation}},
\qquad w_g=\frac{n_g}{N}.
\]
With the sample and outcome fixed, reducing weighted within-cell variance increases between-cell variance: they are the same objective. Population variances use the $n_g$ divisor. Cell sizes supply the weights; a small cell does not receive the same weight as a large one.
