\PageTitle{A16 / CHANNEL HOLDOUT CALCULATIONS}{Acquisition lift\newline and uncertainty in iCAC}{Technical appendix | The acquisition denominator is uncertain even when recorded spending is fixed.}
\Section{Acquisition holdout uncertainty}
\TableCaption{channel-uncertainty}{Acquisition-only pilot estimates with approximate 95\% intervals. Costs are treated as fixed.}
\VAR{channel_interval_table}
\Evidence{The acquisition-rate difference uses an unpooled Bernoulli standard error over all assigned prospects. Scale the interval to treatment size, then invert its positive range for iCAC. A lift interval crossing zero has no finite upper iCAC bound. These are acquisition-cost intervals; the value projection does not claim a joint lifetime-return confidence interval.}

\Section{Interpretation and scope}
Each comparison includes all assigned prospects and its own control. The counts estimate first paid acquisition within the stated 31-day window. The cost numerator includes the focal media and other incremental acquisition costs. There are no experimental contribution, retention or lifetime outcomes in this source.
\Section{The value assumption is separate}
The final planning table joins each acquisition result to a matching operational cohort's contribution value before CAC. It assumes the additional customers have that value and that the campaign does not change the value of customers who would acquire anyway. Varying that value changes the recommendation; iCAC itself is the break-even contribution-value threshold.
